    ramp_envelopes: { cell: "I", ev: "miso-156 (2026-08-13, NO SOLVE) — INERT FOR MISO on a pre-check whose kill rule was fixed BEFORE the numbers were read: the model must OUT-ramp the measured fleet at the p99 1-h move in >=2 of 3 years, else a ramp envelope cannot bind. IT FIRES 0 OF 3. Model P1 thermal dispatch (keeper's committed class_hourly, 12 dispatchable thermal classes) vs measured EIA-930 MISO thermal (NG+COL+OTH, MISO_fueltype.parquet), same fixed-CST clock and non-leap calendar: |1-h move| ratio model/measured at p50/p90/p99 = 0.59/0.73/0.83 (2023), 0.63/0.72/0.83 (2024), 0.66/0.79/0.88 (2025); Jun+Jul p99 0.84/0.81/0.87. MISO's model is SMOOTHER than the real fleet at every quantile of every year — it under-ramps by 12-41 % — so tightening its inter-hour movement would push a fleet that already moves too little further the wrong way and cannot lift a marginal unit. RULE 25 [R-ISO-SCOPE] WORKING AGAINST THE LANE'S OWN INTEREST: PJM's K transfers nothing — pjm-140 armed ramp_limits because its model OUT-ramped the real PJM fleet 1.4-1.7x at p99, and MISO measures the reciprocal. (The 2024 max-ratio 0.17 is one measured 45,174 MW EIA-930 1-h step, an evident reporting artifact; it is reported rather than dropped, and p99 is the quoted statistic precisely because it is insensitive to it.) Tested because miso-156's Phase-0 decomposition landed on branch B-IDENT (marginal-unit identity owns 169.0 % of the 2025 annual C3a gap) and this was the queue's named identity lever. results/calibration/FINDING-miso156-the-gap-is-identity-and-the-fuel-channel-runs-backwards-2026-08-13.md §7.2; probe scripts/probes/_miso156_ramp_precheck.py; record _miso156_ramp_precheck.json miso-219 (2026-09-05, ZERO-SOLVE) RE-EXAMINED THIS CELL AS THE STRONGEST CANDIDATE FOR THE C3a-2025 evening-ramp object AND LEFT THE I UNTOUCHED, on rule 28(a) DO-NOT-REDO. The pull was real: the ramp_limits docstring names this lane's object verbatim ('forces the LP to either pre-position slow CC before the evening ramp or clear fast resources at the ramp margin') and scenarios.py names this lane's diagnosis outright ('a zone-aggregate ungated family clears inertly from ~10 GW of idle evening CC headroom at zero opportunity cost - THE MISO LESSON', issue #1492). It is refused anyway, and miso-219's own measurement is CONSISTENT WITH miso-156 RATHER THAN EVIDENCE AGAINST IT: in the top-15 measured-price Jun-Jul hours the model's thermal fleet runs at 72.3 / 82.7 / 92.8 % of its own annual max against the REAL MISO fleet's 70.0 / 82.4 / 92.4 % (EIA-930 NG+COL+OTH on the same fixed-CST clock, the miso-156 instrument, record complete in all 45 object hours) - within 0.4 pp in every year, i.e. the model is neither under-committed nor idle relative to reality in these hours, so there is nothing for a tighter inter-hour bound to correct. No MISO ramp-envelope artifact exists (only NYISO/CAISO/PJM/ERCOT). PJM's K transfers nothing (rule 25). results/calibration/FINDING-miso219-evening-scarcity-tail-2026-09-05.md; probe scripts/probes/_miso219_evening_tail_phase0.py; record _miso219_evening_tail.json" },
