    zonal_gas_basis: { cell: "K", ev: "ARMED at MISO zonal onboarding gate 2 (docs/multi-iso/miso-zonal-gate2.md, keeper 2026-07-02-miso-38-zonal-reserves, --miso-zonal-gas-basis in its recipe) and carried by every MISO keeper since; the South 2025 row rebuilt at MISO-60 (+0.095 → +0.343 raw, EIA N3045LA3 − HH). RECORD CORRECTION (miso-213, 2026-09-05): this line previously opened \"miso-119 (keeper lineage …)\" — miso-119 adjudicated gas_offer_margin_zonal_anchor (I), never the basis. miso-212 (2026-09-04, ZERO-SOLVE, cell UNCHANGED at K — a rule-19 LAYERING NAMED, not re-verdicted): the increment is added to EVERY gas tranche, including the plants whose F was just set from their own EIA-923 monthly print (gas_plant_monthly_fuel_pricing) — a print that already embeds the regional delivered premium, so two mechanisms price one phenomenon on those plants. Sized on the South idle-within-$20 block in the real S→N RDT-binding hours: +$0.29/MMBtu = $3.1/MWh, 0.78 GW of the 3.27 GW block made economic when removed (2024 0.37 / $1.3; 2023 0.46 / $1.5); Midwest sign −0.20 (under-priced there). NAMED as miso-213 — a MISO-scoped single-delta A/B that skips the increment on 923-priced tranches only (fallback-priced plants keep it: there the basis is the only regional signal). Predicted small and C3a-adverse in the South; structural regardless (rule 1). FINDING-miso212-south-gas-cost-basis-2026-09-04.md §8. miso-213 (2026-09-05, cell UNCHANGED at K — the mechanism is SCOPED, not removed): the layering is REPAIRED by the keeper field miso_zonal_gas_basis_skip_923_priced (K); under the keeper's print path 100 % of MISO gas capacity-hours are print-derived, so in a MISO BACKCAST this mechanism now contributes NOTHING to any gas cell's delivered price (its increment reaches only trajectory cells, i.e. every forecast-mode cell, where it remains the sole regional signal). Its K stands on the forward story and on the code path, not on any backcast effect. FINDING-miso213-zonal-basis-layering-2026-09-05.md." },
    gas_hub_basis_overlay: { cell: "R", ev: "miso-156 (2026-08-13, NO SOLVE) — REJECTED on TWO grounds, NEITHER of them the residual (rule 1 [R-STRUCT]). (1) ITS OWN MEASURED INPUT POINTS THE WRONG WAY: the model's capacity-weighted delivered gas is ABOVE both measured comparators in ALL THREE years — by +0.348/+0.092/+0.892 $/MMBtu against the pre-registered PRIMARY (Henry Hub month + the MISO row of data/raw/gas_basis_by_iso_month.csv, the constrained-hub month spot) and by +1.543/+1.005/+1.010 against a second comparator (EIA delivered-to-electric-power via data/raw/miso_zonal_gas_hub.csv, capacity-weighted across the six carry zones). Arming the overlay would CUT the model's 2025 delivered gas by $0.89/MMBtu; the decomposition's cost-level channel already reads D2 = -6.156 $/MWh (-86.9 % of the 2025 annual C3a gap), i.e. the fuel channel runs BACKWARDS and closing it makes C3a WORSE. (2) RULE 14 [R-ACCURATE]: apply_hub_basis_overlay REPLACES every gas unit's price with one ISO-wide hub-month series, 'superseding both the ISO-month EIA-923 series and the per-plant F923 overwrite' (data/fuel/hubs.py:1268-1272) — right for NEISO (two plants report Schedule-5 receipts) and wrong for MISO, where miso-153 Ground 3 measured the model reproducing across-plant delivered-gas dispersion at 5.321->3.593->3.413 $/MMBtu against an F923 source of 5.657->3.748->3.679. Arming it trades a faithful per-plant measurement for a flat one. Rule 19 [R-ONE-MECH]: MISO's ISO-wide gas LEVEL remains owned by the F923/EIA-923 series alone — the armed miso_zonal_gas_basis is MEAN-ZERO by construction (north/south gradient only) and the armed miso_winter_citygate_daily is Dec/Jan/Feb and mean-preserving within month, so neither can move a June/July level and neither is changed. results/calibration/FINDING-miso156-the-gap-is-identity-and-the-fuel-channel-runs-backwards-2026-08-13.md §7.1; PREREG-miso156-c3a-three-channel-decomposition-2026-08-13.md (162a51d, blob 7f124d18); _miso156_c3a_decomposition.json. SCARCE-SET CORROBORATION miso-189 (2026-08-30, NO SOLVE — verdict UNCHANGED, this is a second citation, not a re-test): the owner-item-(6) MISO-Illinois $8.22/MMBtu scarce delivered-gas observation phase-0'd on a pre-declared rule and REFUTED as a C3a-2025 lever — on the 2025 scarce set (47 h) the model's Illinois book sits $4.2/MMBtu ABOVE the best of six measured comparators (F923 IL burn-weighted 4.05 vs book 8.218) while the matched-plant grain reproduces the reporting plants' own F923 prints to -0.33, i.e. ground (1)'s backwards fuel channel holds scarce-set-scoped and ground (2)'s per-plant fidelity is measured directly at the scarce grain; the dispersion is real (near-idle IL CTs' own prints: Venice 11.2-12.4, Goose Creek 13.9-15.4, Raccoon Creek 20.5-22.7 $/MMBtu on 2.3-2.8k MMBtu/month takes). Residue (ADVERSE sign, owner-court, cross-ISO): the marginal-vs-average delivered-cost question — repricing near-idle CTs at marginal commodity cost (~$3-4) would LOWER scarce prices. FINDING-miso189-illinois-scarce-gas-phase0-2026-08-30.md; _miso189_illinois_gas_phase0.json. miso-212 (2026-09-04, ZERO-SOLVE, cell UNCHANGED at R — the owner-court residue is now SIZED): in the real S→N RDT-binding Jun–Jul hours the South gas idle-within-$20 block carries F − HH spot = +$0.84/MMBtu ($8.7/MWh), of which the 923 print itself is +$0.55; repricing every South tranche at HH spot would make 2.10 / 2.03 / 1.56 GW (2025 / 2024 / 2023 shoulders) of the 3.27 GW block economic at the model's South price — the single largest measured leg and over the miso-212 G-5 trigger, NOT armed because it is a cost CONVENTION (average delivered → marginal commodity) that is ISO-wide (Midwest prints +$0.73 over HH vs Chicago Citygate −$0.26) and cross-ISO. Owner question, stated in FINDING-miso212 §8: should gas offers carry the EIA-923 average delivered cost or marginal commodity + variable transport? A ruling FOR is a spec change with a cross-ISO A/B program, not a MISO lane lever; AGAINST closes the item." },
